  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B014",
        "laureates": [
          "Christopher Jarzynski",
          "Denis J. Evans",
          "Gavin E. Crooks"
        ],
        "citation": "for foundational fluctuation theorems governing nonequilibrium systems"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B028",
    "B025"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "The fluctuation theorems identify a clean and unexpectedly universal mechanism: even far from equilibrium, fluctuations obey exact quantitative relations connecting work, entropy production, and reversibility. Evans, Jarzynski, and Crooks represent the principal successive formulations that made this insight both general and usable. The results have endured theoretical examination and experimental tests, and they now organize stochastic and quantum thermodynamics rather than remaining an isolated formal observation.",
    "case_against": "The historical boundary between the Evans results, the Jarzynski equality, Crooks relations, and later experimental verification needs exact adjudication. Moreover, the theorems are theoretical results whose public visibility and technological consequences are less immediate than those of several instrumental candidates.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson regards circuit QED as a mature, controllable realization of strong light-matter coupling, while noting that credit for the full architecture reaches beyond three people.",
        "response": "I agree that circuit QED is an important and durable experimental platform. The unresolved breadth of attribution is nevertheless consequential, and its significance is closely entangled with the still-developing quantum-computing programme. B014 has a more sharply delimited set of exact physical relations and an unusually coherent three-person attribution."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll emphasizes the exceptional reproducibility and fundamental-measurement power of optical lattice clocks, with Katori's concept and Ye's experimental advances forming a clear pairing.",
        "response": "This is the strongest alternative because the attribution and maturity are excellent. I would qualify the case by asking whether the achievement should be cited as a distinct discovery rather than the culmination of precision-spectroscopy development; the ultimate redefinition of the second also remains a future metrological decision. The fluctuation theorems more directly establish a new general law of nonequilibrium physics."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson argues that dynamical mean-field theory became a foundational and quantitatively useful framework for correlated materials, while recognizing its distributed intellectual lineage.",
        "response": "I share the high assessment of DMFT as a powerful theory, especially its ability to reduce a difficult many-body problem to a tractable self-consistent description. But its character as an approximation framework and the diffuse priority between infinite-dimensional, formal, and practical developments make a limited Nobel attribution less clean than for B014."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the precise historical allocation of priority among Evans, Jarzynski, and Crooks supports the proposed three-person citation without material omissions.",
      "Whether the committee judges the experimental testing of fluctuation theorems sufficiently broad for a prize centered on a theoretical nonequilibrium framework."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
